Falsifies `inter_process_communication::verification::a_collected_sender_is_forgotten`.

The defect: the collected sender is put back on the queue it came from, so collecting it is a
peek rather than a take. This is a design error with an argument behind it rather than a typo:
the caller is blocked and something has to be able to find it again, and leaving it queued looks
like the cheapest way to keep it findable. DECISIONS §12 (a one-shot reply capability) is what
says otherwise: after the collect, the kernel-minted Reply is meant to be the only name for that
thread anywhere.

Expected to fail: the harness's `assert!(e.senders.is_empty() && e.receivers.is_empty())` after
a `FromSender`, and the second receive would produce the same caller again, which is a second
name for a thread that is supposed to be replied to exactly once.

diff --git a/crates/inter_process_communication/src/lib.rs b/crates/inter_process_communication/src/lib.rs
--- a/crates/inter_process_communication/src/lib.rs
+++ b/crates/inter_process_communication/src/lib.rs
@@ -347,2 +347,4 @@ impl<T: Node> Rendezvous<T> {
         } else if let Some(sender) = self.senders.pop_front() {
+            // SAFETY: just popped from this queue, so it is valid and on no queue.
+            unsafe { self.senders.push_back(sender) };
             Receive::FromSender(sender)
